WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:26.019 --> 00:00:29.899
Ferdinand Magellan was one of the leading
explorers of the Age of Discovery.

00:00:29.899 --> 00:00:35.570
Often cited as the first man to circumnavigate
the globe, he fell just short of that milestone,

00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:39.420
being hacked to death by natives on the Marianas
Islands.

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:44.370
Yet, his vision and his tenacity, despite
tremendous odds, opened up the westward route

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:48.510
to Asia and established his legacy as one
of history’s great adventurers.

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:57.260
In today’s Biographics, we uncover the life
and tragic death of Ferdinand Magellan.

00:00:57.260 --> 00:01:00.690
Beginnings

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:04.120
Ferdinand Magellan was born around 1480 in
the north of Portugal.

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:06.350
His family were part of the Portuguese aristocracy.

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:10.170
His father Rui de Magalhaes, was an official
in the city of Aveiro, while the family of

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:14.530
his mother, Alda de Mesquita, were well connected
to the Portuguese royal court.

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:18.869
Both parents died when Ferdinand was about
ten years of age and he was taken into the

00:01:18.869 --> 00:01:20.570
royal court as a page boy.

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:26.760
Ferdinand was page to Queen Leonor, wife of
King John II who reigned from 1481-1495.

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:29.770
He was then succeeded by his brother, King
Manuel I.

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:33.939
At the beginning of the 16th Century, just
as Ferdinand was coming of age, Portugal was

00:01:33.939 --> 00:01:36.040
the most nautically advanced country in Europe.

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:40.840
She had the best built ships and the most
experienced sailors on the high seas.

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:44.390
These advances had allowed the Portuguese
to take the lead in exploring the African

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:45.390
west coast.

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:49.450
However, the Italian states had already established
land-based trade routes to the East, shutting

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:52.249
Portugal out from this lucrative market.

00:01:52.249 --> 00:01:56.179
Portuguese businessmen and explorers sought
ways to develop their own route to the East

00:01:56.179 --> 00:01:58.859
that would bypass the established land-based
route.

00:01:58.859 --> 00:02:02.799
And so, the rush was on to find a seaborne
route to the eastern markets.

00:02:02.799 --> 00:02:07.509
Portuguese explorer Vasco Da Gama made a huge
breakthrough when he sailed down the African

00:02:07.509 --> 00:02:12.160
coast and around the Cape of Good Hope and
then to the southern tip of India.

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:17.510
Over the ensuing decade the Portuguese established
trade relations with Indian Kingdoms.

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:21.680
During Ferdinand’s formative years, exploration
and trade opportunities were constant topics

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:24.120
of interest among the Portuguese nobility.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:31.630
The Crown was fully invested in this enterprise
and was prepared to finance explorations.

00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:35.330
Mariner

00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:39.970
Around 1505, Ferdinand, now in his mid-twenties,
enlisted as a member of a twenty-two-ship

00:02:39.970 --> 00:02:44.850
escort for Francisco de Almeida, the first
viceroy of Portuguese India.

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:48.920
It is probable that he also travelled to such
places as Malaysia and the African territory

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:49.920
of Mozambique.

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:54.030
In the midst of this flourishing inter-cultural
trade, he would have been exposed to a dizzying

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:58.280
array of languages, cultures, foods and trade
goods.

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:01.500
Magellan remained in the employ of the Portuguese
navy for eight years.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:06.710
He was involved in a number of battles, the
first being the Battle of Cannanore in 1506.

00:03:06.710 --> 00:03:10.760
In this encounter the Portuguese fleet of
less than ten ships defeated in excess of

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:14.850
more than two hundred much smaller Indian
vessels.

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:17.040
Magellan was wounded in this encounter.

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:21.570
In February, 1509, Magellan participated in
the Battle of Diu against the Ottoman Turks

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:24.960
in southwestern India, which was another Portuguese
victory.

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:28.550
A year later, he was present when the Indian
city of Goa was overrun.

00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:32.920
Having negated the Muslim push into Asia,
the Portuguese turned their attention to Malaysia

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:34.930
and the city of Malacca.

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:39.200
This island city was an international port
that served as a gateway to the lucrative

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:40.950
spice markets to the south.

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:46.390
The Portuguese wanted control over the city
and sent an invasion force to achieve it in

00:03:46.390 --> 00:03:47.390
1511.

00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:50.350
Magellan was among the invaders, who soon
conquered the island.

00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:54.760
Towards the end of 1511, Magellan was awarded
for his services with an award of riches from

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:57.820
an exploratory mission to the Spice Islands
of Indonesia.

00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:00.720
He was also given a Malay slave, who he named
Enrique.

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:08.330
This man was to act as a translator on Magellan’s
later voyage to the Indian Ocean.

00:04:08.330 --> 00:04:11.920
Soldier

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:13.880
Magellan returned to Portugal in 1513.

00:04:13.880 --> 00:04:17.489
He had spent eight years on the seas of the
Indies.

00:04:17.489 --> 00:04:22.940
He returned to a country that was deeply involved
both militarily and commercially in the African

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:23.940
continent.

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:28.169
Conflicts were ongoing both with North African
pirates and the Muslims who inhabited the

00:04:28.169 --> 00:04:29.169
continent.

00:04:29.169 --> 00:04:32.770
Shortly after returning to Portugal, Magellan
joined an expedition against a revolt in the

00:04:32.770 --> 00:04:34.630
Moroccan city of Azamor.

00:04:34.630 --> 00:04:38.150
Morocco had been a possession of the Portuguese
since 1486.

00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:41.840
As a result, each of its cities were obligated
to pay an annual tribute.

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:46.629
However, in 1513, the ruler of Azamor, a man
by the name of Moulay Zamon, refused to pay.

00:04:46.629 --> 00:04:50.289
He then led a revolt against the Portuguese,
with the goal of pushing the invader out of

00:04:50.289 --> 00:04:51.289
the country.

00:04:51.289 --> 00:04:53.830
Back in Spain, the king determined to put
down the rebellion with overwhelming force

00:04:53.830 --> 00:04:56.210
in order to teach his subjects there a lesson.

00:04:56.210 --> 00:05:00.319
He sent an army of 15,000 men under the leadership
of the Duke of Braganza.

00:05:00.319 --> 00:05:02.520
This force soon quashed the uprising.

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:06.409
Within a few years, however, the Portuguese
found that the costs involved in keeping order

00:05:06.409 --> 00:05:10.610
in Azamor were more than the revenue received
from the tribute and the city was abandoned.

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:14.009
During the Battle of Azamor, Magellan was
thrown from his horse, receiving a leg wound

00:05:14.009 --> 00:05:16.860
that would leave him with a limp for the remainder
of his life.

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:20.169
This was not the only unhappy experience he
had in Morocco.

00:05:20.169 --> 00:05:24.370
As a prominent member of the victorious army,
he was awarded some of the spoils of victory.

00:05:24.370 --> 00:05:28.460
But, before long, he was accused of carrying
on illegal trade with locals by selling some

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:31.720
of those spoils back to the Moroccans at a
profit.

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:35.930
The accusation was thoroughly investigated
by Magellan’s military superiors and he

00:05:35.930 --> 00:05:37.770
was found to be not guilty.

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:43.090
Still the episode left a stain on his, up
until then, exemplary record.

00:05:43.090 --> 00:05:46.730
It seems to have lost him considerable favour
with King Manuel, whose sister he had served

00:05:46.730 --> 00:05:48.120
as a page boy in his youth.

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:51.629
On his return to Lisbon, Magellan requested
an increase in his pension on the strength

00:05:51.629 --> 00:05:53.479
of his service in Morocco.

00:05:53.479 --> 00:05:54.960
This was denied by the king.

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:59.020
When he requested permission to accompany
several other Portuguese military expeditions,

00:05:59.020 --> 00:06:00.389
Magellan was again turned down.

00:06:00.389 --> 00:06:07.729
It was apparent that he had lost the trust
of his monarch.

00:06:07.729 --> 00:06:10.020
Explorer

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:14.249
With the king leaving him out in the cold,
Magellan now set about formulating plans for

00:06:14.249 --> 00:06:16.050
his own voyage of exploration.

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:20.419
His intention was to find a faster route to
the Spice Islands by sailing west across the

00:06:20.419 --> 00:06:21.499
Atlantic Ocean.

00:06:21.499 --> 00:06:25.530
He was unaware of the size of the Pacific
Ocean or the length of the South American

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:29.400
continent, and so imagined that this would
be quicker and easier than sailing all the

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:33.060
way down to the southern tip of Africa to
get to the Indian Ocean.

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:35.999
Magellan took his plan to the king, requesting
financing for the expedition.

00:06:35.999 --> 00:06:39.809
He used the fact that the Spanish were likely
to reach the Spice Islands before them and

00:06:39.809 --> 00:06:43.169
claim jurisdiction over its valuable spice
trade.

00:06:43.169 --> 00:06:48.499
However, King Manuel was not moved and he
turned down Magellan’s request, He did,

00:06:48.499 --> 00:06:51.729
however, give permission to seek funding from
other rulers.

00:06:51.729 --> 00:06:55.470
Magellan set his sights on the Spanish crown
as the backer for his expedition.

00:06:55.470 --> 00:06:59.779
He travelled to Seville where he met up with
other Portuguese mariners and navigators.

00:06:59.779 --> 00:07:02.719
Together they formulated a plan to take to
the Spanish crown.

00:07:02.719 --> 00:07:06.939
The expedition would be presented as the opportunity
to achieve the westward route to the Indies

00:07:06.939 --> 00:07:10.029
that Columbus had failed to achieve in the
1490’s.

00:07:10.029 --> 00:07:13.729
This new route would also avoid competition
with the Portuguese who were committed to

00:07:13.729 --> 00:07:17.409
their well established, but much longer, route
through the Indian Ocean.

00:07:17.409 --> 00:07:21.509
As icing on the cake, Magellan contended that,
on the basis of cartography calculations,

00:07:21.509 --> 00:07:25.879
the Spice Islands would be found to lie within
territory that was the possession of the Spanish

00:07:25.879 --> 00:07:26.879
under the Treaty of Tordesillas of 1494.

00:07:26.879 --> 00:07:29.189
The proposal was attractive to Spanish King
Charles Ist.

00:07:29.189 --> 00:07:33.279
Magellan and his cartographer, Rui Faleiro,
were made joint captains of the expedition.

00:07:33.279 --> 00:07:36.869
They were also made Knights of the Order of
Santiago and given a ten-year monopoly on

00:07:36.869 --> 00:07:40.849
all trade in the area through their route
along with 5 percent of all revenues received

00:07:40.849 --> 00:07:41.849
from the route.

00:07:41.849 --> 00:07:44.499
As a bonus, they would each be given governorship
of an island.

00:07:44.499 --> 00:07:48.710
The Spanish Crown put up the money for the
purchase of ships and crew and supplies.

00:07:48.710 --> 00:07:51.460
This was a stunning success for Magellan.

00:07:51.460 --> 00:07:57.099
Yet, despite the financing and prospects conferred
by the King, he soon ran into difficulties.

00:07:57.099 --> 00:08:01.710
Spanish sailors were incensed that two Portuguese
mariners had been placed at the head of the

00:08:01.710 --> 00:08:02.710
expedition.

00:08:02.710 --> 00:08:05.779
When they heard of all of the lucrative benefits
that the two would receive, they were angrier

00:08:05.779 --> 00:08:06.779
still.

00:08:06.779 --> 00:08:10.169
Members of the Spanish court, unable to stomach
the king’s generosity to these foreigners,

00:08:10.169 --> 00:08:12.009
attempted to subvert the expedition.

00:08:12.009 --> 00:08:16.249
The releasing of funds was held up, resulting
in a flow on effect that made it virtually

00:08:16.249 --> 00:08:18.869
impossible for the two captains to make their
preparations.

00:08:18.869 --> 00:08:23.430
Meanwhile, King Manuel Ist, back in Portugal,
heard about the Spanish financing of the voyage.

00:08:23.430 --> 00:08:28.119
This apparently caused him to feel regret
that he had turned down the request by Magellan.

00:08:28.119 --> 00:08:32.560
He set about trying to sabotage the Spanish
expedition, sending an envoy to the Spanish

00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:35.050
court for that express purpose.

00:08:35.050 --> 00:08:37.560
Problems developed too, between Magellan and
Faleiro.

00:08:37.560 --> 00:08:41.360
In fact, the relationship between them became
so strained that Faleiro had his captaincy

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:42.880
revoked.

00:08:42.880 --> 00:08:44.790
He was kicked off the expedition.

00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:46.350
More personnel problems followed.

00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:49.970
The Spanish prevented Magellan from bringing
Portuguese sailors on board, despite the fact

00:08:49.970 --> 00:08:53.650
that many of the men that Magellan wanted
were experienced in sailing the parts of the

00:08:53.650 --> 00:08:54.980
ocean that he was heading to.

00:08:54.980 --> 00:08:58.430
In the end, the majority of the crew were
Spanish, although there was representation

00:08:58.430 --> 00:09:01.140
from a number of other lands, including North
Africa.

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:03.252
Magellan’s slave, Enrique also sailed with
his master.

00:09:03.252 --> 00:09:08.410
A Fateful Voyage

00:09:08.410 --> 00:09:13.240
Finally, Magellan had five ships ready to
sail.

00:09:13.240 --> 00:09:18.270
These were the Trinidad, upon which he sailed,
the Victoria, the Concepcion, the Santo Antonio

00:09:18.270 --> 00:09:19.450
and the Santiago.

00:09:19.450 --> 00:09:22.300
In total, there were 273 men.

00:09:22.300 --> 00:09:26.500
Everything we know about Magellan’s voyage
comes from a journal that was kept by a crewmember

00:09:26.500 --> 00:09:28.910
by the name of Antonio Pigafetta.

00:09:28.910 --> 00:09:33.700
They sailed out of Seville harbor on St. Lawrence
Day, August 10th, 1519.

00:09:33.700 --> 00:09:37.630
During his time in Seville, Magellan had met
and married a teenage girl by the name of

00:09:37.630 --> 00:09:39.950
Beatriz Barbosa, who was the daughter of a
Portuguese friend.

00:09:39.950 --> 00:09:44.690
Not long thereafter, Beatriz, gave birth to
their first and only child, a son named Rodrigo.

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:48.330
On sailing out of Seville, he would never
see them again.

00:09:48.330 --> 00:09:50.870
The fleet sailed down the Guadalquivir river
and the port of Sanlucar de Barrameda.

00:09:50.870 --> 00:09:53.900
Finally, on September 20th, they headed out
into the Atlantic Ocean.

00:09:53.900 --> 00:09:56.290
The first stop was Tenerife in the Canary
Islands.

00:09:56.290 --> 00:10:00.410
Getting there was plain sailing but, the next
leg of the journey, from Tenerife southwest

00:10:00.410 --> 00:10:03.950
across the equator was beset by wild storms.

00:10:03.950 --> 00:10:08.200
The chronicler Pigafetta describes many occasions
where the men thought they were done for as

00:10:08.200 --> 00:10:10.340
terrible storm winds battered the ships.

00:10:10.340 --> 00:10:14.910
He puts their deliverance down to the divine
interference of Saint Anselm, who showed his

00:10:14.910 --> 00:10:17.250
presence by a light at the tip of the mast.

00:10:17.250 --> 00:10:21.720
What he was actually seeing was St. Elmo’s
Fire, an electrical charge that is often seen

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:23.850
on the top of masts in thunderstorms.

00:10:23.850 --> 00:10:27.880
Having navigated the stormy seas, Magellan
set a course for Brazil, which was a possession

00:10:27.880 --> 00:10:28.880
of the Portuguese.

00:10:28.880 --> 00:10:31.770
But they had not yet established any substantial
settlements there.

00:10:31.770 --> 00:10:35.410
Magellan’s five ships reached the Brazilian
coast in early November and then moved south

00:10:35.410 --> 00:10:37.470
until they reached the bay of Rio De Janairo.

00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:41.800
Having reached South America, Magellan had
to face the reality of the geography that

00:10:41.800 --> 00:10:43.440
he had only previously guessed at.

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:47.470
He had assured the Spanish king that he would
discover a strait through this continent that

00:10:47.470 --> 00:10:52.390
would allow them to pass into the South Seas
and then make a direct run to the East Indies.

00:10:52.390 --> 00:10:56.200
What he found was that the South American
coast ran for further to the south than he

00:10:56.200 --> 00:10:59.640
had ever imagined – and there was no promise
that he would find a strait to cut through

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:00.640
it.

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:03.200
The realization that they might be forced
to add an unknown length of time to their

00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:07.270
journey by circumnavigating South America
caused tension among the crew.

00:11:07.270 --> 00:11:10.590
From Rio De Janairo, the fleet travelled south
to the Rio de la Platta estuary.

00:11:10.590 --> 00:11:13.470
There they stopped to allow the men to explore
inland.

00:11:13.470 --> 00:11:18.850
Here they came across a tribe of natives,
described by Pigafetta as cannibalistic giants.

00:11:18.850 --> 00:11:22.290
According to his journal account, the crew
managed to coax four of the natives aboard

00:11:22.290 --> 00:11:23.350
one of the ships.

00:11:23.350 --> 00:11:25.950
While distracting them with trinkets, Pigafetta
writes . . .

00:11:25.950 --> 00:11:28.650
The captain had the fetters put on the feet
of both of them.

00:11:28.650 --> 00:11:32.900
And when they saw the bolt across the fetters
being struck with a hammer to rivet and prevent

00:11:32.900 --> 00:11:35.520
them from being opened, these giants were
afraid.

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:39.030
But the captain made signs to them that they
should suspect nothing.

00:11:39.030 --> 00:11:42.570
Nevertheless, perceiving the trick that had
been played on them, they began to blow and

00:11:42.570 --> 00:11:46.950
foam at the mouth like bulls, loudly calling
on Setebos (their devil) to help them.

00:11:46.950 --> 00:11:49.420
Magellan put the other two in irons but then
decided to release them.

00:11:49.420 --> 00:11:53.680
The actions of the sailors appear excessively
aggressive toward the native people of Rio

00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:55.010
de la Platta.

00:11:55.010 --> 00:11:57.570
It quickly devolved into outright combat.

00:11:57.570 --> 00:12:01.410
Magellan lost his first man when a sailor
was hit in the torso by a poisoned arrow.

00:12:01.410 --> 00:12:02.930
Magellan made a speedy exit.

00:12:02.930 --> 00:12:07.010
But, rather than head back out into the open
seas, it was decided to head into the river

00:12:07.010 --> 00:12:11.420
in the belief that it could just be the strait
that would allow them to cut across the continent.

00:12:11.420 --> 00:12:14.860
It didn’t take them long, however, to realize
that the waters they were travelling were

00:12:14.860 --> 00:12:19.280
freshwater and so could not have come from
the ocean on the other side of the continent.

00:12:19.280 --> 00:12:23.090
When Magellan realized his mistake, he turned
south, coming to the port of San Julian in

00:12:23.090 --> 00:12:25.660
Argentina just prior to Easter, 1520.

00:12:25.660 --> 00:12:28.190
He decided to lay up there over the winter.

00:12:28.190 --> 00:12:30.260
The rest would not be without incident.

00:12:30.260 --> 00:12:35.120
A mutiny was incited by two Spanish captains
who had resented serving under a Portuguese

00:12:35.120 --> 00:12:36.140
from the start.

00:12:36.140 --> 00:12:39.890
Magellan had the loyalty of the majority of
the men and the dissenters were sidelined

00:12:39.890 --> 00:12:40.890
and dismissed.

00:12:40.890 --> 00:12:44.360
During the layover, the Santiago headed off
on a reconnaissance mission to the south.

00:12:44.360 --> 00:12:46.510
It became shipwrecked in the Santa Cruz River.

00:12:46.510 --> 00:12:49.000
The ship was lost along with one of the crew
members.

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:53.270
Just before we get continue to Magellans journey,
let me take a moment to tell you about Squarespace,

00:12:53.270 --> 00:14:03.910
who makes these longer videos possible!

00:14:03.910 --> 00:14:22.060
The Straits of Magellan

00:14:22.060 --> 00:14:23.730
Magellan was now down to four ships.

00:14:23.730 --> 00:14:27.630
After picking up the crew from the Santiago,
they carried on down the South American coast

00:14:27.630 --> 00:14:32.310
to Cape Virgenese.There they found a Strait
between the continental mainland and the island

00:14:32.310 --> 00:14:33.480
of Tierra del Fuego.

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:37.640
It was the strait that they were looking for
to take them across to the far ocean and would

00:14:37.640 --> 00:14:40.120
come to be known as the Straits of Magellan.

00:14:40.120 --> 00:14:44.070
One ship was sent forth to see whether the
salt water would turn to fresh water as they

00:14:44.070 --> 00:14:45.130
progressed through the strait.

00:14:45.130 --> 00:14:48.810
It didn’t and the entire fleet headed inland.

00:14:48.810 --> 00:14:50.960
Before long they came to a fork in the waters.

00:14:50.960 --> 00:14:55.080
Magellan sent the Concepcion and the Santo
Antonio along the southeast channel while

00:14:55.080 --> 00:14:58.930
his ship, the Trinidad, and the Victoria,
headed south west.

00:14:58.930 --> 00:15:02.930
During this exploration, many of the crew
of the Santo Antonio mutinied and the ship

00:15:02.930 --> 00:15:04.210
headed back for Spain.

00:15:04.210 --> 00:15:07.330
The remaining three ships figured that the
westward channel was the one to follow and

00:15:07.330 --> 00:15:10.760
so they headed down it on November 30th, 1529.

00:15:10.760 --> 00:15:15.440
By December 1st they had made it to the South
Sea, which Magellan rechristened the Pacific

00:15:15.440 --> 00:15:16.440
Ocean.

00:15:16.440 --> 00:15:20.440
Getting through the Straits was an impressive
feat of maritime skill, withy many treacherous

00:15:20.440 --> 00:15:22.210
areas to be navigated.

00:15:22.210 --> 00:15:25.710
While Magellan was ecstatic to have found
the passage that he’d been seeking, he was

00:15:25.710 --> 00:15:29.440
also very aware that he was running desperately
low on supplies.

00:15:29.440 --> 00:15:32.390
Stopping to forage along the coast was an
option, but this brought with it the risk

00:15:32.390 --> 00:15:34.640
of further conflict with native peoples.

00:15:34.640 --> 00:15:39.340
As a result, the decision was made to push
directly into the ocean, despite the lack

00:15:39.340 --> 00:15:40.340
of food.

00:15:40.340 --> 00:15:42.940
They had no idea what they were in for.

00:15:42.940 --> 00:15:45.960
It would be another twelve weeks before land
would be sighted.

00:15:45.960 --> 00:15:49.330
The food situation soon became desperate with
Pigafetta recording . . .

00:15:49.330 --> 00:15:54.010
We only ate old biscuit reduced to powder,
and full of grubs, and we drank water that

00:15:54.010 --> 00:15:56.030
had turned yellow and smelled.

00:15:56.030 --> 00:16:00.440
When even the biscuits were gone, they caught
and ate rats – and then were reduced to

00:16:00.440 --> 00:16:02.480
chewing on leather cloth and sawdust.

00:16:02.480 --> 00:16:06.200
Scurvy became rampant and the death toll began
to mount.

00:16:06.200 --> 00:16:09.410
Among the first to die were the two giants
who had been captured a few months earlier.

00:16:09.410 --> 00:16:11.210
At least thirty of the crew followed.

00:16:11.210 --> 00:16:16.410
Finally, at the end of January 1521, a group
of uninhabited small islands were sighted.

00:16:16.410 --> 00:16:20.190
Finding no food there, they pushed on until
they came to the island of Guam at the beginning

00:16:20.190 --> 00:16:21.190
of March.

00:16:21.190 --> 00:16:24.550
On this inhabited island they finally found
food and supplies.

00:16:24.550 --> 00:16:29.750
From Guam, the fleet headed southwest to the
Marianas Islands, which we know as the Philippines.

00:16:29.750 --> 00:16:33.120
Magellan was keen to establish friendly relations
with the native people there.

00:16:33.120 --> 00:16:37.250
Thanks to the aid of his translator, Enrique,
he was able to establish diplomatic relations

00:16:37.250 --> 00:16:44.370
through the giving of gifts, demonstrations
of arms and tours of the ships.

00:16:44.370 --> 00:16:47.910
Journey’s End

00:16:47.910 --> 00:16:51.420
The people of Limasawa Island, on which the
fleet had landed, encouraged them to move

00:16:51.420 --> 00:16:54.300
on to what appeared to be the capital island
of Cebu.

00:16:54.300 --> 00:16:58.320
This they did, but unsure of the reception
they would receive, Magellan ordered the artillery

00:16:58.320 --> 00:17:00.120
to be fired as they entered the port.

00:17:00.120 --> 00:17:02.900
This proved unnecessary as they people were
friendly.

00:17:02.900 --> 00:17:06.890
Magellan formed the opinion that the chief
of this island, Rajah Humabon was the king

00:17:06.890 --> 00:17:08.199
of all of the surrounding islands.

00:17:08.199 --> 00:17:12.520
So, in securing this friendship, he imagined
that he now had the allegiance of all the

00:17:12.520 --> 00:17:13.520
islands.

00:17:13.520 --> 00:17:15.289
In this though, he was mistaken.

00:17:15.289 --> 00:17:18.100
Each island operated as an independent sovereignty.

00:17:18.100 --> 00:17:22.880
Still, Magellan headed for the island of Mactan,
believing that here too he would get a positive

00:17:22.880 --> 00:17:23.880
reception.

00:17:23.880 --> 00:17:27.850
However, the chief of Mactan, Lapu Lapu refused
to submit to the strange foreigners.

00:17:27.850 --> 00:17:30.220
Magellan was offended and withdrew back to
Cebu.

00:17:30.220 --> 00:17:32.880
There he prepared his men to attack the inhabitants
of Lapu Lapu.

00:17:32.880 --> 00:17:37.030
Possibly on the intelligence of Rajah Humabon,
he was convinced that he would not encounter

00:17:37.030 --> 00:17:38.660
a large force of resistors.

00:17:38.660 --> 00:17:40.300
He was wrong.

00:17:40.300 --> 00:17:44.160
When Magellan and his force of about sixty
men waded ashore on Macatan they were confronted

00:17:44.160 --> 00:17:50.059
by between 1500-3000 warriors armed with spears
and arrows.

00:17:50.059 --> 00:17:54.920
The sailors had the benefit of muskets but
these were cumbersome, slow to load and unreliable.

00:17:54.920 --> 00:17:59.520
Hoping to cause confusion, Magellan sent some
men to burn the huts of the natives.

00:17:59.520 --> 00:18:02.290
However, they were soon intercepted and killed.

00:18:02.290 --> 00:18:06.120
Magellan’s attacking forces were unable
to gain any ground, being forced to fight

00:18:06.120 --> 00:18:07.140
from the shore.

00:18:07.140 --> 00:18:11.220
According to Pigafetta, the natives focused
on their attention on Magellan, the man who

00:18:11.220 --> 00:18:12.990
was the leader of the invaders.

00:18:12.990 --> 00:18:17.650
He described the end of his captain . . .
So many of them charged down on us that they

00:18:17.650 --> 00:18:20.440
shot the captain through the right leg with
a poisoned arrow.

00:18:20.440 --> 00:18:22.750
On that account, he ordered us to retire slowly.

00:18:22.750 --> 00:18:27.600
But the men took to flight, except six to
eight of us who remained with the captain.

00:18:27.600 --> 00:18:31.220
The natives shot only at our legs, for the
latter were bare; and so many were the spears

00:18:31.220 --> 00:18:35.310
and stones that they hurled at us, that we
could offer no resistance . . . The natives

00:18:35.310 --> 00:18:39.590
continued to pursue us, and picking up the
same spear four or six times, hurled it as

00:18:39.590 --> 00:18:41.220
us again and again.

00:18:41.220 --> 00:18:45.230
Recognizing the captain, so many turned upon
him that they knocked his helmet off his head

00:18:45.230 --> 00:18:48.130
twice, but he always stood firmly like a good
knight.

00:18:48.130 --> 00:18:51.710
An Indian hurled a bamboo spear into the captain’s
face, but the latter immediately killed him

00:18:51.710 --> 00:18:54.210
with a lance, which he left in the Indian’s
body.

00:18:54.210 --> 00:18:58.400
Then, trying to lay hand on sword, he could
draw it out but halfway, because he had been

00:18:58.400 --> 00:19:00.410
wounded in the arm with a bamboo spear.

00:19:00.410 --> 00:19:03.030
When the natives saw that they all hurled
themselves upon him.

00:19:03.030 --> 00:19:05.370
One of them wounded him in the left leg with
a large cutlass.

00:19:05.370 --> 00:19:09.730
. .That caused the captain to fall face downward,
when immediately they rushed upon him with

00:19:09.730 --> 00:19:15.491
iron and bamboo spears and with their cutlasses
until they killed our mirror, our light, our

00:19:15.491 --> 00:19:17.590
compass and our true guide.

00:19:17.590 --> 00:19:21.500
When they wounded him, he turned back many
times to see whether we were all in the boats.

00:19:21.500 --> 00:19:26.490
Thereupon, beholding him dead, we, wounded,
retreated as best we could, to the boats,

00:19:26.490 --> 00:19:28.110
which were already pulling off.

00:19:28.110 --> 00:19:32.400
The ship made a speedy departure to Cebu,
only to find that Rajah Humabon was no longer

00:19:32.400 --> 00:19:33.400
friendly.

00:19:33.400 --> 00:19:35.890
He had around thirty of the sailors poisoned
during a feast.

00:19:35.890 --> 00:19:38.930
After that, the surviving men set sail for
the Spice Islands.

00:19:38.930 --> 00:19:44.480
The goal of reaching those Islands was finally
met, but Magellan had paid the ultimate price

00:19:44.480 --> 00:19:45.610
in its achievement.

00:19:45.610 --> 00:19:49.660
More than 90 percent of his men also perished
on that fateful voyage.

00:19:49.660 --> 00:19:52.770
The captains of the two remaining ships disagreed
about the route home.

00:19:52.770 --> 00:19:56.800
As a result they parted ways, with the Trinidad
sailing back across the Pacific, while the

00:19:56.800 --> 00:19:59.880
Victoria took the Portuguese route around
the Cape of Good Hope.

00:19:59.880 --> 00:20:03.750
As it turned out, the Trinidad was stopped
by Portuguese officials, with its crew being

00:20:03.750 --> 00:20:04.750
arrested.

00:20:04.750 --> 00:20:08.770
The Victoria arrived back in Seville, Spain
on September 6, 1522.

00:20:08.770 --> 00:20:13.441
The ship was laden down with spices but the
crew were near death from starvation and the

00:20:13.441 --> 00:20:15.870
ship was about to fall apart.

00:20:15.870 --> 00:20:22.680
Only 18 men were onboard from a total of 237
who had set sail from that port three years

00:20:22.680 --> 00:20:23.680
earlier.

00:20:23.680 --> 00:20:28.610
But those 18 men had the distinction of being
the first to circumnavigate the globe

